<section class="section drawing-guide" id="read-a-septic-drawing" aria-labelledby="drawing-guide-heading" data-reading-guide="septic-as-built-records">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="drawing-guide__header">
            <span class="eyebrow">Inside a returned agency file</span>
            <h2 id="drawing-guide-heading">What can you actually read from a septic drawing?</h2>
            <p>We requested a Tennessee agency file and reviewed its historical permit and layout. Here are the recorded findings, explained in plain English.</p>
            <nav class="record-learning__contents" aria-label="Drawing reading guide"><a href="#layout-case">Read the investigation</a><a href="#drawing-labels">Follow the labels</a><a href="#drawing-comparison">Compare the pages</a><a href="#drawing-checklist">Check your file</a></nav>
        </header>
        <article class="record-learning__story" id="layout-case" data-reading-milestone="layout-case">
            <span class="eyebrow">Actual record investigation · Tennessee</span>
            <h3>The property listing could not answer where the system was drawn.</h3>
            <p>The public property information did not supply the tank and drain-field positions. Knowing that a home is described as using septic is different from having its layout.</p>
            <p>We assembled the property identifiers for a focused TDEC request. We asked for the permit, layout, final approval and repair records rather than treating an empty public search as proof that no record existed.</p>
            <p>TDEC returned a historical construction permit and sketch. We separated the written system specifications from the drawing, then delivered the unmodified agency file alongside a two-page explanation. The numbers below describe this returned historical record, not a new measurement of the property.</p>
            <p><strong>The answer:</strong> a three-bedroom design, a 900-gallon tank, and three field lines labeled 60, 80 and 85 feet—225 linear feet in total. The sketch places the tank beside the house and shows the field-line arrangement. That gave the customer a documented starting point that the listing had not supplied.</p>
            <p><strong>The unresolved part:</strong> the sketch has no north arrow and is not a scaled survey. It does not establish the exact present-day position, later changes, or working condition. We did not turn a recovered historical layout into a claim that the system had been located on site.</p>
            <p class="record-learning__note">This was a completed records delivery, not a completed site inspection. Source basis: saved customer request, TDEC return, historical permit and customer delivery. Personal identifiers are omitted; the displayed excerpt is the existing public crop.</p>
        </article>
        @template.tags.recordFindingVisual(variant = "roane")
        <div class="drawing-guide__layout">
            <figure class="drawing-guide__figure">
                <a href="/images/case-study/tn-historical-sketch-anonymized.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open the anonymized historical septic sketch at full size" data-reading-image="historical-sketch" data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="learning_drawing_enlarge" data-track-target-type="public_image">
                    <img src="/images/case-study/tn-historical-sketch-anonymized.png" width="1110" height="560" loading="lazy" alt="Historical septic sketch with a house, tank beside the house, and three dashed field lines marked 60, 80, and 85 feet">
                </a>
                <figcaption>Original agency scan, with identifying header and road label cropped out. Open the image to enlarge it. The accompanying construction permit is not, by itself, proof of completed installation.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <dl class="drawing-guide__reading" id="drawing-labels">
                <div><dt>House and tank</dt><dd>The sketch places a small tank beside the house. This is a historical location clue; confirm its position before excavation or construction.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Field lines</dt><dd>The three dashed runs are labeled 60, 80, and 85 feet. Together they total 225 feet of field line. That is a length, not a drain-field area.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Facts on a different page</dt><dd>The accompanying permit lists a 900-gallon tank and a three-bedroom design. Those details come from the permit text, not from measuring this sketch.</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <article class="record-learning__story" id="drawing-comparison" data-reading-milestone="drawing-comparison">
            <span class="eyebrow">From a mark on the page to a supported answer</span>
            <h3>Read the labels before making the picture tell a story.</h3>
            <p>Start at the house in the scan. The small rectangle beside it is the tank symbol; the dashed runs extend into the field. The written lengths are evidence you can read. The apparent size of each shape on your screen is not a reliable measurement.</p>
            <p>Next, separate what the drawing says from what another page says. Here, the line lengths come from the sketch; the tank capacity and design bedrooms come from the accompanying permit. A useful explanation keeps that distinction visible so another reader can check it.</p>
            <p>The final question is what kind of record this is. This public example includes a construction permit. Even a clear drawing needs installation or closeout evidence before it can support a statement about what was completed. A newer repair file could add another chapter.</p>
        </article>
        <div class="record-learning__comparison" aria-label="How to compare pages in a septic file">
            <div><strong>Drawing</strong><p>Identify the legend, labeled lengths and relative arrangement. Do not guess missing dimensions.</p></div>
            <div><strong>Permit or approval</strong><p>Read the document's stated use, capacity, date and conditions. Check that it belongs to the same property and project.</p></div>
            <div><strong>Later record</strong><p>Look for revisions, installation notes and repair files. Explain a conflict before choosing one number as the answer.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="drawing-guide__types">
            <h3>A proposed plan and an as-built answer different questions</h3>
            <dl class="record-search-facts">
                <div><dt>Construction permit or approved plan</dt><dd>Describes authorized work. Check for installation, final approval, or operation records before treating it as the completed system.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>As-built or installation record</dt><dd>Documents the installed arrangement. Check its date, property match, and later alterations; it still does not establish today's condition.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Unlabeled site sketch</dt><dd>Ask the file owner whether it shows proposed or installed work. A drawing found in the packet is not automatically an as-built.</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>
        <article class="record-learning__checklist" id="drawing-checklist">
            <h3>Five questions to ask of your own packet</h3>
            <ol><li>Does every page match the same property and project?</li><li>Is the drawing proposed, installed, revised, or unlabeled?</li><li>Which dimensions are written, and which am I only estimating visually?</li><li>Do the permit, drawing and later records agree?</li><li>Which questions still need the records office or a field professional?</li></ol>
            <p>If two pages disagree, preserve both and describe the difference. Ask whether one superseded the other; do not average the values or quietly choose the more convenient one.</p>
        </article>
        <div class="drawing-guide__next">
            <div>
                <h3>What if your file only contains the permit?</h3>
                <p>Ask for the installation drawing or as-built, final inspection or operation approval, and later repair records. Specify the missing document rather than requesting the same permit again.</p>
                <a href="/septic-permit-records-request/">See what to include in a records request</a>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>Want help connecting the pages?</h3>
                <p>Send the property and what you need to establish. We can investigate the file and explain what the documents confirm and what remains unanswered.</p>
                <a class="button button--primary" href="/offer-prep-septic-file-check/#record-help" data-record-help-cta data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="as_built_drawing_help" data-track-target-type="lead_form">Ask SepticPath to review the record</a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="drawing-guide__provenance">Case source: the historical construction-permit packet and agency reply shown in our <a href="/#home-case-title">completed Tennessee request</a>. Personal identifiers are excluded. The example explains one returned file and does not establish another property's permit status.</p>
        <p class="drawing-guide__provenance">For the basic tank-to-drainfield sequence, see <a href="https://www.epa.gov/septic/how-septic-systems-work" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">EPA's explanation of septic systems</a>. For a separate request made while preparing a property for sale, <a href="/septic-record-brief-example/#sale-case" data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="learning_drawing_brief" data-track-target-type="internal_page">read the missing-permit case and its completed Record Brief</a>.</p>
    </div>
</section>
